chnique, tactics, and context\n\nPassing platform control, setting accuracy, approach timing, arm swing, blocking footwork, and serving technique determine whether the system functions under pressure.\n\nTeams adjust serving targets, block schemes, defensive depth, tempo, setter distribution, substitutions, and attacking combinations based on opponent tendencies.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nMetrics include side-out percentage, serve pressure, reception quality, attack efficiency, block touches, transition conversion, errors, and rotation-specific performance.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPractice moves from technical repetitions into unit coordination, serve-receive patterns, live transition, rotation-specific scenarios, and pressure scoring.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nNet contact rules, centre-line restrictions, court spacing, landing awareness, equipment standards, and concussion or injury protocols protect players.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nVolleyball systems improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Volleyball systems: How performance is measured","word_count":586,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Volleyball systems"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIVB","title":"FIVB","url":"https://www.fivb.com/volleyball/the-game/official-volleyball-rules/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-075","published_at":"2026-04-12T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"volleyball-systems-what-beginners-often-misunderstand-075","title":"Volleyball systems: What beginners often misunderstand","description":"Volleyball systems explained through what beginners often misunderstand, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Volleyball systems: What beginners often misunderstand\n\nVolleyball systems is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on what beginners often misunderstand while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nVolleyball systems coordinate serve receive, setting, attacking, blocking, floor defence, transition, and rotation responsibilities across six players.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## What beginners often misunderstand\n\nBeginners often mistake visible effort for effective performance, or assume that one technique, drill, or metric has universal value. In volleyball systems, outcomes come from the interaction of skill, decision-making, physical capacity, rules, and context. Passing platform control, setting accuracy, approach timing, arm swing, blocking footwork, and serving technique determine whether the system functions under pressure.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nPassing platform control, setting accuracy, approach timing, arm swing, blocking footwork, and serving technique determine whether the system functions under pressure.\n\nTeams adjust serving targets, block schemes, defensive depth, tempo, setter distribution, substitutions, and attacking combinations based on opponent tendencies.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nMetrics include side-out percentage, serve pressure, reception quality, attack efficiency, block touches, transition conversion, errors, and rotation-specific performance.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPractice moves from technical repetitions into unit coordination, serve-receive patterns, live transition, rotation-specific scenarios, and pressure scoring.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nNet contact rules, centre-line restrictions, court spacing, landing awareness, equipment standards, and concussion or injury protocols protect players.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nVolleyball systems improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Volleyball systems: What beginners often misunderstand","word_count":587,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Volleyball systems"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIVB","title":"FIVB","url":"https://www.fivb.com/volleyball/the-game/official-volleyball-rules/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text,
  published_at timestamptz,
  slug text,
  title text,
  description text,
  body_markdown text,
  article_type text,
  language text,
  featured_image_url text,
  featured_image_alt text,
  word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer,
  keywords text[],
  provider text,
  model text,
  image_source_type text,
  image_provider text,
  image_model text,
  refs jsonb
);

update public.source_items si
set status='processed',
    rejection_reason=null,
    instructions=coalesce(si.instructions,'{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true
    )
from _blogdel_sports_payload p
where si.external_id=p.external_id
  and si.category_id=(select id from public.categories where slug='sports');

update public.articles a
set slug=p.slug,
    title=p.title,
    description=p.description,
    body_markdown=p.body_markdown,
    article_type=p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,
    language=p.language,
    status='published'::public.article_status_enum,
    featured_image_url=p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt=p.featured_image_alt,
    word_count=p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes=p.reading_time_minutes,
    keywords=p.keywords,
    provider='chatgpt',
    model='gpt-5.6-sol',
    is_demo=false,
    event_at=p.published_at,
    published_at=p.published_at,
    archived_at=null,
    generation_job_id=null,
    image_source_type=p.image_source_type,
    image_provider=p.image_provider,
    image_model=p.image_model,
    updated_at=now()
from _blogdel_sports_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
where a.source_item_id=si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id, category_id, author_id, generation_job_id,
  slug, title, description, body_markdown, article_type, language,
  status, featured_image_url, featured_image_alt, word_count,
  reading_time_minutes, keywords, provider, model, is_demo,
  event_at, published_at, image_source_type, image_provider, image_model
)
select
  si.id, si.category_id, au.id, null,
  p.slug, p.title, p.description, p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum, p.language,
  'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url, p.featured_image_alt, p.word_count,
  p.reading_time_minutes, p.keywords,
  'chatgpt','gpt-5.6-sol',false,
  p.published_at,p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,p.image_provider,p.image_model
from _blogdel_sports_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join lateral (
  select id from public.authors
  where category_id=si.category_id and is_active
  order by last_used_at asc nulls first
  limit 1
) au on true
where not exists (select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_sports_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id
  and a.source_item_id=si.id
  and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id, source_item_id, provider, title, url, authority, position, retrieved_at
)
select
  a.id, si.id,
  ref->>'provider', ref->>'title', ref->>'url',
  nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
  coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer, ordinality::integer-1),
  now()
from _blogdel_sports_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref,ordinality);

commit;
